<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy — what this app collects, tracks, and reaches for.

  History
    2026-08-07  A. Sigdel  Created.
    2026-08-08  A. Sigdel  Argued the calendar, which is the first thing read off
                           the phone rather than out of the conversation.

  All four lists are deliberately empty and each will stay that way or not for a
  stated reason.

  Nothing is collected: a conversation goes to the provider because that is what
  answering it requires, and nowhere else. That is not collection by Apple's
  definition — no data leaves for this developer — so declaring a data type here
  would be wrong rather than cautious.

  The calendar is the first thing that tests that sentence, and it is worth
  arguing rather than waving through. An event read by `read_calendar` becomes a
  tool result, a tool result becomes a message, and the next request carries it
  to the provider. So calendar contents do leave the device.

  They still are not collected. The definition turns on who receives the data for
  their own purposes, and the recipient here is the model answering the turn the
  person asked — the same path their typed question already takes, for the same
  reason, and no copy is kept by or sent to this developer. A person who does not
  want their calendar in that request declines the permission, and the tool
  reports the refusal rather than working around it.

  What would change this is a second destination: anything that logs, caches off
  the device, or analyses a turn for a purpose other than answering it. None
  exists, and the first one to be proposed is the point at which this list gains
  an entry rather than this paragraph gaining a sentence.

  No accessed-API categories yet. Two are already visible on the way: the file
  tools will read modification dates, which is NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp,
  and anything persisting settings brings NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults.
  Adding either before the code that needs it would claim a reason nothing backs.
  Keychain is not a required-reason API and needs no entry.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array/>
</dict>
</plist>
